window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["03"]={"https://theconversation.com/rural-australian-communities-are-hosting-the-clean-energy-boom-are-they-getting-a-fair-say-296254":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"6130755cad5e554683eca3dce3c7099c3fd5067bc1788168e9e1fe80cd50d7a3","read":"https://theconversation.com/rural-australian-communities-are-hosting-the-clean-energy-boom-are-they-getting-a-fair-say-296254","bytes":185320,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Rural Australian communities are hosting the clean energy boom. Are they getting a fair say?","by":[{"n":"Simon Wright","r":"Senior Research Fellow, Energy & Circularity, Gulbali Institute, Charles Sturt University"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T19:05:21Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Australia’s transition to clean energy is happening at pace, with regional areas front and centre. Rural communities are being asked to host the wind farms, solar panels and transmission lines to power our biggest cities – sometimes hundreds of kilometres away."]},{"k":"p","s":["Accommodating these large developments can be burdensome for small communities. Projects can impact farmland and load local roads. They can also pile more pressure on housing and essential services."]},{"k":"p","s":["To sweeten the deal, affected communities are being promised a once-in-a-generation wave of investment, touted as an economic lifeline for many regional towns ",["struggling to survive","https://theconversation.com/the-migration-debate-has-a-big-blind-spot-much-of-regional-australia-is-shrinking-293692"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Not everyone is buying the promise. With a ",["state election looming","https://theconversation.com/politics-with-michelle-grattan-kos-samaras-and-richard-willingham-on-victorias-expected-change-of-government-296250"],", Victoria offers some stark examples of how these tensions are playing out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some councils have been ",["sidelined","https://www.weeklytimesnow.com.au/news/victoria/councils-blindsided-as-state-government-rams-through-planning-blitz/news-story/5be7e1e3f2fe9d7eeceef000a823cd42"]," by the state government in key decisions. Planning laws have been ",["changed","https://www.weeklytimesnow.com.au/news/victoria/councils-blindsided-as-state-government-rams-through-planning-blitz/news-story/5be7e1e3f2fe9d7eeceef000a823cd42"]," to fast-track projects. In many areas, ",["community backlash","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-10/central-north-rez-scrapped-dookie-renewable-energy-government/107136588"]," has been fierce."]},{"k":"p","s":["These tensions point to questions that go beyond any individual project. Are regional communities being given a meaningful say in their economic future? And will they actually be left better off?"]},{"k":"h","s":["Big changes are coming"]},{"k":"p","s":["For regional Australia, the scale of change is enormous. So, too, is the economic opportunity it presents."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Australian Energy Market Operator has ",["estimated","https://reneweconomy.com.au/households-lead-march-to-least-cost-electricity-as-aemo-issues-call-to-arms-on-renewable-transition/"]," that achieving net zero emissions will require $106 billion in new investment (in today’s dollars) by 2050. This includes investment in 6,000 kilometres of new transmission lines across the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["This money is already flowing. According to the ",["Clean Energy Council","https://cleanenergycouncil.org.au/news-resources/h1-2026-large-scale-renewable-generation-and-storage"],", 141 clean energy projects worth about $32.5 billion were financed or already under construction in the first half of this year."]},{"k":"h","s":["A noisy, confusing picture"]},{"k":"p","s":["One of the biggest challenges for communities is navigating this rapidly evolving landscape. Each state and territory is taking a different approach to these developments – not only for renewable energy but also for critical minerals and mining more broadly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take Victoria, for example. VicGrid and the Department of Energy, Environment and Climate Action recently released ",["community engagement and social value guidelines","https://www.vicgrid.com.au/community/community-engagement-and-social-value-guidelines"],". These set minimum expectations for engagement with and benefits for communities and landowners who host projects, but (unlike in New South Wales) no minimum dollar-per-megawatt figure."]},{"k":"p","s":["The guidelines only cover renewable energy and transmission projects, but exclude projects connecting to the local distribution network. Separate compensation arrangements apply to some transmission projects, adding to the complexity."]},{"k":"p","s":["Initially voluntary, these guidelines only become mandatory in mid-2027, when the extent of collaboration between developers will also be assessed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Importantly, and perhaps confusingly, they do not cover critical mineral projects. These are covered by separate legislation and still-to-be-determined community benefit payments."]},{"k":"p","s":["In short, it is a noisy, confusing and dynamic landscape for communities and landowners."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet some regional communities are taking matters into their own hands and electing to shape their own energy destiny. In north-west Victoria, for example, Wimmera Southern Mallee Development has been working with energy developers and various government agencies through its ",["Renewable Energy Collaboration","https://www.infrastructure.gov.au/territories-regions/regional-australia/regional-renewables-community-showcase/wimmera-southern-mallee-renewable-energy-collaboration"]," partnership."]},{"k":"h","s":["Not everyone is on board"]},{"k":"p","s":["Victoria again offers some striking examples of vocal community opposition."]},{"k":"p","s":["There’s been sustained backlash from farmers against the Victoria to NSW Interconnector West (VNI West) transmission project in north-west Victoria, which the state opposition have ",["vowed to scrap","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-08-19/victorian-coalition-promises-to-scrap-vni-west-transmission-line/107053638"]," if elected."]},{"k":"p","s":["And in the north of the state, intense community backlash recently convinced the government to ",["abandon a plan","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-10/central-north-rez-scrapped-dookie-renewable-energy-government/107136588"]," to create a new renewable energy zone around Dookie."]},{"k":"p","s":["This month, Australian Public Policy Institute ",["research","https://appi.org.au/report/social-licence-and-benefit-sharing-in-the-shift-to-renewable-energy-in-regional-communities/"]," (of which I was a coauthor) casts light on some of these concerns for rural Australia more broadly."]},{"k":"p","s":["This drew on workshops and interviews conducted with a broad range of stakeholders in three towns in regional NSW impacted by renewable energy development. We found affected communities broadly support the shift to clean energy."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, key community concerns are already delaying the process. This threatens to to undermine the long-term energy transition."]},{"k":"h","s":["Why communities are wary"]},{"k":"p","s":["Assessing these responses, we found three big challenges for the government and industry."]},{"k":"p","s":["First, community consultation and engagement were seen as inconsistent and often inadequate. Further, there were limited opportunities for communities, including First Nations people, to meaningfully influence project planning and decision-making."]},{"k":"p","s":["Second, communication and planning processes lacked transparency, clarity and a place-based focus."]},{"k":"p","s":["And third, communities are experiencing significant social and economic impacts. Benefit-sharing and compensation arrangements were viewed as inequitable, insufficiently targeted and lacking long-term community value."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bridging all of these challenges, we found trust in industry, government and the planning system was fragile."]},{"k":"h","s":["What communities want"]},{"k":"p","s":["Encouragingly, however, we also found regional participants in NSW were keen to provide detailed policy solutions for governments to address many of these challenges."]},{"k":"p","s":["They called for four key changes."]},{"k":"p","s":["First, minimum, enforceable engagement standards should be required for all major energy projects. These standards should prioritise transparency, accountability and building trust."]},{"k":"p","s":["Second, governments should work with communities to develop a framework on sharing of benefits. This should then be embedded into planning regulations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Third, there should be more support and resources for communities to help with negotiating how project benefits are shared."]},{"k":"p","s":["And fourth, at all levels of government, planning processes and governance should adopt a place-based approach tailored to local needs."]},{"k":"h","s":["Building trust"]},{"k":"p","s":["Increasingly for Australia, the main barriers to a successful shift to renewable energy are not technical but social."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many rural and regional communities feel excluded from decisions about how the transition will be achieved. Importantly, communities are not rejecting the transition. They are asking for better information and communication, fairer benefits, and a genuine role in shaping the changes that affect them."]}],"chars":6338},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cape-verde":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"620c285b14d56913f058b994dfe6b60a236c77c89e775d9e8beba44b37a6806d","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/cape-verde","bytes":56437,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Cape Verde travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-02-04T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-07-28T16:13:41+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"p","s":["Global Health Insurance Cards (GHIC) are not accepted in Cape Verde."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cape-verde/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Cape Verde’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Cape Verde set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Cape Verdean Consulate-General in Rotterdam","https://conscv.nl/pt/"]," (in Portuguese) or through the ",["Cape Verde Consular Portal email address","https://portalconsular.mnec.gov.cv/en/contactos"]," (in English)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Cape Verde, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost, stolen or is damaged."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual Citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can enter Cape Verde as a visitor for up to 30 days without a visa."]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer or to travel for other reasons, you must ",["meet the Cape Verde government’s entry requirements","https://portalconsular.mnec.gov.cv/en/vistos"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Traveller entry form"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must fill in a ",["traveller entry form","https://www.ease.gov.cv/"]," at least 5 days before you arrive in the country. Complete the pre-registration and pay the airport security fee (‘TSA’) on the official online platform. Bring proof of confirmation to show when you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["From 1 July 2026, if you do not complete the pre-registration or cannot show proof, you will have to pay double the TSA fee at border control."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling with a tour operator, check if they can do this for you."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["more information on the traveller entry form","https://portalconsular.mnec.gov.cv/en/perguntas-frequentes"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Children aged one and under do not need to have a completed form and are exempt from the TSA fee."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"p","s":["Children aged 17 and under travelling to Cape Verde alone, or with a person who is not their parent or legal guardian, must carry a letter of authorisation to travel from their parent or guardian. The letter must be in Portuguese and the signature must be certified by a ",["solicitor","https://solicitors.lawsociety.org.uk/"]," or ",["notary","https://www.facultyoffice.org.uk/notaries/find-a-notary/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The letter should include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the child’s dates of arrival and departure"]},{"k":"li","s":["the address where they will be staying"]},{"k":"li","s":["the name of the adult in Cape Verde who will be responsible for them during their stay"]},{"k":"li","s":["the full names, passport numbers and contact details of the parent or guardian"]},{"k":"li","s":["the parent or guardian’s signature and date of issue"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are not the parent or guardian and you bring a child to Cape Verde without a legally certified letter of authorisation, you may be refused entry."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Cape Verde, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Cape Verde guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/43/cape-verde#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Cape Verde. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Cape Verde"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Cape Verde, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["The crime rate in Cape Verde is generally low, but can include pickpocketing, bag snatching, burglary and muggings, particularly in the capital city of Praia and on the islands of Sal and Boa Vista. Sexual assaults are rare but have been reported."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can take steps to protect yourself and your belongings, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["leaving valuables somewhere secure, such as a hotel safe"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping your belongings in sight at bars and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["not carrying large amounts of cash or wearing expensive jewellery"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoiding unlit and isolated areas after dark"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoiding beaches with limited or restricted visibility"]},{"k":"li","s":["making sure your hotel or accommodation’s windows and doors are always locked"]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are severe penalties for possessing, using or trafficking drugs. Penalties include fines and long prison sentences."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Buying property"]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals have experienced problems when buying property in Cape Verde. Before buying property on the islands, get independent qualified legal advice. See FCDO’s list of ",["English-speaking lawyers in Cape Verde","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Cape%20Verde"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Beaches and swimming"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are going into the water, always use beaches where there is a lifeguard on duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tides and currents around the islands are very strong. Take care when walking close to the water’s edge, especially along unsupervised stretches of beach. Waves can be unpredictable and have a strong undertow. Follow the lifeguard’s instructions and respect the warning flags:"]},{"k":"li","s":["red means danger: do not go into the water"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow means caution: you can walk in the water, but you cannot swim"]},{"k":"li","s":["green means it’s safe to swim"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Cape Verde, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a valid UK photocard driving licence to drive in Cape Verde for up to 6 months. If you still have a paper driving licence, you should update it to a photocard licence, or get the ",["1968 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," and carry it with your licence. You will be arrested if you are found driving without a licence."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hiring vehicles"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you hire a car, scooter or quad bike, make sure it’s in good condition and that it’s fully insured. Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"p","s":["Quad biking is considered an extreme sport. It is unlikely that it will be included in your travel insurance policy. Check whether you need additional insurance to cover extreme sports and make sure it covers you for the cost of medical care and repatriation. Medical facilities are very basic in Cape Verde so it is highly likely that following a quad bike accident you will need to be medically evacuated."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s a legal requirement to wear a crash helmet when riding a scooter or quad bike. Your insurance policy may be invalidated if you have an accident and you’re not wearing a helmet."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Public transport"]},{"k":"p","s":["Only hire authorised taxis from taxi ranks or by asking your hotel to call one. On the islands of Sal and Boa Vista, authorised taxis are coloured blue and yellow."]},{"k":"p","s":["Intercity bus services can be dangerous because of poor driving."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sea conditions around Cape Verde can be dangerous. Follow local advice before travelling by sea. Travel by sea to the southern islands of Brava and Fogo in particular can often be disrupted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["The rainy season in Cape Verde is from mid-August to mid-October. Torrential rains on some of the islands can cause floodings and landslides. Monitor local weather reports and expect difficulties when travelling to affected areas during this season."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Seismic activity and tremors can be felt occasionally, mostly on the islands of Brava and Fogo. This is usually within the bounds of normal activity but can sometimes be dangerous. Check with your travel provider before travelling to the islands of Brava or Fogo."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sandstorms"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sandstorms (‘bruma seca’) can happen between December and February. When intense, they can disrupt air travel, especially on the island of Boa Vista. If a sandstorm happens while you’re on one of the islands, contact your tour operator or airline."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need. Health care in Cape Verde is very basic and limited"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant. Global Health Insurance Cards (GHIC) are not accepted in Cape Verde so it is essential that you have appropriate travel insurance."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 130 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the current ",["vaccine recommendations for Cape Verde","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/43/cape-verde#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on ",["the NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face on Cape Verde","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/43/cape-verde"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["shigella sonnei"]},{"k":"li","s":["salmonella"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have an underlying health condition, speak to a healthcare professional before travelling."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Health Security Agency has investigated an increase in cases of Shigella sonnei and Salmonella in travellers returning from Cape Verde. Reported cases have decreased, but there is still a risk of gastrointestinal illness as in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can reduce your risk by practising good hand hygiene and following food and water precautions. Read more about ",["food and water hygiene on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/44/food-and-water-hygiene"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Cape Verde government has raised the civil protection warning level for dengue on the islands of Santiago, Fogo and Brava."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk on the island of Fogo in Cape Verde. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Changes to your routine when travelling, such as later nights, coping with heat and changes to your diet can have an impact on your medication. See the ",["Summer Proof Your Health","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/heat-travel-and-late-nights-how-summer-can-affect-your-medicines-and-how-to-stay-safe"]," campaign for more information."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medication is only available from pharmacies. Find a nearby pharmacy using the ",["list of pharmacies in Cape Verde","https://emprofac.cv/informacoes-uteis/rede-farmacias?qt-rede_farmacias=3#qt-rede_farmacias"]," (in Portuguese)."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you take prescribed medication, bring enough for the duration of your visit and keep a few days’ supply in your hand luggage in case your checked baggage is delayed or lost."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have a UK prescription and run out of medication, ask a pharmacy whether they can supply it. The same medicine, strength or dose may not be available, and some medicines may be in short supply or unavailable in Cape Verde."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need a repeat prescription, go to the nearest health centre or hospital emergency department. You may have to pay for your medication."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Cape Verde"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities in Cape Verde are basic and limited, and some medicines are in short supply or unavailable. The largest hospitals are in Praia on Santiago and Mindelo on São Vicente, with smaller medical facilities and clinics located throughout the country. Medical facilities are particularly limited on the island of Boa Vista."]},{"k":"p","s":["The islands of Brava and Santo Antão do not have working airports, so medical air evacuation is extremely difficult."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Cape Verde","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cape-verde-list-of-medical-facilities-and-practitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Cape Verde"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 130"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 131"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 132"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=cape%20verde"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=cape%20verde"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=cape%20verde"]," in Cape Verde"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Cape Verde","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-information-for-cape-verde"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Cape Verde","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/cabo-verde-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/cape-verde/british-embassy-lisbon"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/cape-verde/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":14203,"change":"Updated information about travelling with medication (‘Health’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/revisi-ruu-penyiaran-pengaturan-dahulu-pembungkaman-kemudian-293209":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:47Z","sha":"b6c399c16ca783ea1cb8edf199f6a00e154649f43b7d0c3d428bcd1c9801632f","read":"https://theconversation.com/revisi-ruu-penyiaran-pengaturan-dahulu-pembungkaman-kemudian-293209","bytes":175920,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Revisi RUU Penyiaran: pengaturan dahulu, pembungkaman kemudian?","by":[{"n":"Dicki Mahardika","r":"Podcast Producer, The Conversation Indonesia"},{"n":"Mufti Nurlatifah","r":"Dosen Ilmu Komunikasi Universitas Gadjah Mada"}],"pub":"2026-10-01T02:00:55Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Bagaimana jika aktivitas live streaming, rekaman podcast atau konten video yang diunggah secara bebas tiba-tiba harus melewati perizinan yang rumit serta harus menghadapi ancaman sensor berlapis dari pemerintah?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Wacana ",["revisi Rancangan Undang-Undang Penyiaran","https://www.remotivi.or.id/headline/meja-redaksi/871"]," kembali memicu keresahan publik, karena dinilai menyimpan pasal-pasal bermasalah yang mengancam ekosistem digital Indonesia. Selain itu, di balik alasan pembaruan aturan penyiaran yang sudah usang, draf regulasi ini menyimpan ambiguitas besar yang berpotensi mencederai kebebasan berekspresi."]},{"k":"p","s":["Dalam episode terbaru podcast SuarAkademia kali ini, kami mengajak Mufti Nurlatifah, akademisi dari Departemen Ilmu Komunikasi Universitas Gadjah Mada Yogyakarta untuk menyoroti masalah mendasar dari RUU Penyiaran, yaitu kegagalan dalam membedakan antara logika penyiaran tradisional (broadcasting logic) dan logika media digital (digital logic)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mufti menjelaskan bahwa regulasi penyiaran konvensional sejak awal dirancang untuk mengelola frekuensi publik yang jumlahnya terbatas. Namun dalam draf RUU Penyiaran, pemerintah berupaya menyamaratakan aturan frekuensi publik tersebut ke dalam ruang digital seperti Youtube, TikTok, layanan OTT seperti Netflix, hingga podcast di platform streaming audio."]},{"k":"p","s":["Menurut Mufti, pencampuradukan dua logika yang berbeda ini dinilai tidak akomodatif dan tidak sesuai dengan karakter ekosistem digital."]},{"k":"p","s":["Selain itu, Mufti juga menjelaskan bahwa tumpang tindih regulasi akan menjadi dampak tak terelakkan jika draf ini disahkan tanpa revisi substansial. Saat ini, para kreator konten dan platform digital telah terikat oleh aturan berlapis, mulai dari mekanisme self-regulation, pedoman komunitas platform, UU ITE, hingga UU Perlindungan Anak. Sehingga, ketika RUU Penyiaran memaksakan kewajiban pendaftaran, izin siar, dan pengawasan konten digital, maka akan timbul birokrasi yang gemuk dan ketidakpastian hukum."]},{"k":"p","s":["Niat berbagi edukasi atau berbagi pandangan kritis malah justru berisiko terjerat aturan hukum yang membingungkan karena tidak jelasnya batasan antara siaran langsung penyiaran dan konten digital biasa."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ketidakpastian hukum tersebut berpotensi memicu iklim selfcensorship atau sensor mandiri yang represif. Menurut Mufti, ketakutan terhadap sanksi atau pemblokiran malah akan memaksa kreator konten maupun penyedia platform untuk bersikap defensif."]},{"k":"p","s":["Platform digital kemungkinan akan memperketat penyaringan kata kunci secara otomatis dan membatasi jangkauan konten sensitif demi menghindari konflik dengan negara. Hal ini bisa berakibat semakin terancamnya ruang publik untuk berdiskusi, memberikan kritik, serta mengekspresikan gagasan secara bebas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Mufti juga mempertanyakan tentang perluasan jangkauan pengawasan KPI ke ranah digital, mengingat rekam jejak kinerjanya yang kerap menuai kritik publik. KPI pernah melakukan sensor berlebihan yang tidak sesuai tempatnya pada tayangan kartun anak, tetapi di sisi lain tetap membiarkan penayangan sinetron yang dinilai tidak mendidik."]},{"k":"p","s":["Simak episode lengkapnya hanya di SuarAkademia ngobrol seru isu terkini, bareng akademisi."]}],"chars":3066},"https://theconversation.com/the-state-apologised-to-abuse-in-care-survivors-two-years-ago-today-what-has-changed-296136":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T17:03Z","sha":"52b7c8a4c5ebabe100948f55aa78879e76cd16daaf6d1608aeb78ae05fa89f5d","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-state-apologised-to-abuse-in-care-survivors-two-years-ago-today-what-has-changed-296136","bytes":180519,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The state apologised to abuse-in-care survivors two years ago today. What has changed?","by":[{"n":"Stephen Winter","r":"Associate Professor in Political Theory, University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau"}],"pub":"2026-11-11T17:26:38Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Today marks two years since New Zealand’s government ",["formally apologised","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/533547/the-full-text-of-christopher-luxon-s-crown-apology-to-abuse-survivors"]," to survivors of abuse in out-of-home care."]},{"k":"p","s":["It came in response to ",["the Royal Commission of Inquiry into Abuse in Care","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/"],", which exposed decades of systemic abuse and neglect in state and faith-based institutions between 1950 and 1999, affecting some 200,000 people."]},{"k":"p","s":["In all, the commission ",["made more than 200 recommendations","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/23121/whanaketia-part-8.pdf"]," aimed at transforming the care system and providing meaningful redress to survivors."]},{"k":"p","s":["The apology, delivered in parliament by Prime Minister Christopher Luxon, was accompanied by a commitment to mark the subsequent anniversary as a “ ",["Day of Reflection","https://www.beehive.govt.nz/release/national-day-reflection-survivors-abuse-neglect-care"],"” – assessing what has, and has not, been accomplished for survivors."]},{"k":"p","s":["Two years on, some important progress has been made. But gaps remain between what the commission recommended and what survivors have received."]},{"k":"h","s":["A legacy of trauma"]},{"k":"p","s":["The commission’s final report, released in July 2024, made for appalling reading."]},{"k":"p","s":["Care was under-resourced and poorly monitored, with children subjected to torture at institutions such as ",["Lake Alice Psychiatric Hospital","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/reports/inquiry-into-the-lake-alice-child-and-adolescent-unit"],". Māori and Pacific people faced systemic racism, including abuse targeting their ethnicity and separation from their families, language and culture."]},{"k":"p","s":["For many survivors, the personal and social consequences have been profound. Their experiences resulted in lifelong trauma, poor health, relationship difficulties and poverty."]},{"k":"p","s":["New Zealand society continues to experience the resulting ",["care-to-crime cycle","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/our-progress/library/v/500/care-to-custody-incarceration-rates-research-report"],". Out-of-home care often meant disrupted education, weakened family and community ties and the normalisation of violence. In short, care was ",["criminogenic","https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1177/1473225416669145"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The commission ",["found that up to 80% of current prisoners","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0023/23891/he-purapura-ora-he-mara-tipu-vol_1_web.pdf"]," had spent time in state care. Its ",["report","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/reports/whanaketia"]," on Hokio Beach School and Kohitere Boys’ Training Centre found vulnerable young people were subjected to abuse and neglect, with little support to address their trauma. Many instead found protection, connection and belonging in gangs."]},{"k":"p","s":["For many whānau, out-of-home care became an inter-generational cycle, as the children of those impoverished and incarcerated were taken into care in succession."]},{"k":"h","s":["Progress made, but problems remain"]},{"k":"p","s":["To transform New Zealand’s failing care system, the commission proposed ",["a decade-long shift away","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/reports/whanaketia/part-9/chapter-9"]," from state care towards local, community and hapū-based support, alongside a new puretumu torowhānui (holistic redress) system for survivors."]},{"k":"p","s":["These reforms were intended to work together. Adequate redress could help survivors rebuild their lives and break inter-generational cycles of care, while a transformed care system would provide better support for those in need."]},{"k":"p","s":["Encouragingly, the commission’s work has led to some significant improvements."]},{"k":"p","s":["Survivors now have access to ",["Kōnae","https://www.konae.org.nz/"],", an information clearing house for care records, and the government-funded ",["Survivor Experiences Service","https://survivorexperiences.govt.nz/"],". ",["State Redress New Zealand","https://www.redress.govt.nz/"]," provides a single point of application for compensation, replacing a fragmented process with ",["a common practice framework","https://www.redress.govt.nz/about-redress-improvements/improve-redress-offerings-alignment-and-consistency-across-the-system/about-the-common-payment-framework"]," and more transparent assessment standards."]},{"k":"p","s":["More funding has been allocated, with average payments rising from around $19,000 to $30,000 and top-ups offered for previously settled claims."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet these important gains must be weighed against serious shortcomings with the government’s response."]},{"k":"p","s":["The government did not adopt the commission’s flagship recommendation to establish an independent redress body. Survivors must instead seek redress from the very institutions responsible for their abuse, which effectively judge their own cases – deciding which harms warrant compensation."]},{"k":"p","s":["Survivors of abuse in faith-based care are also excluded from state redress, leaving them subject to ",["differing","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/reports/from-redress-to-puretumu/from-redress-to-puretumu-4/1-1-introduction-9/1-1-introduction-23"]," – and sometimes inadequate – processes."]},{"k":"p","s":["As more survivors to come forward, they are overwhelming an under-resourced system."]},{"k":"p","s":["In June 2020, the Ministry of Social Development had ",["a backlog of 2,235 claims","https://www.abuseincare.org.nz/__data/assets/pdf_file/0023/23891/he-purapura-ora-he-mara-tipu-vol_1_web.pdf"],". By June 2026, there were ",["6,654 outstanding","https://www.redress.govt.nz/redress-publications"],", with some survivors ",["facing waits of up to five years","https://www.redress.govt.nz/assets/State-Redress/Implementation-Plan_digital-for-online.pdf"],". By comparison, ",["a similar process in Canada","https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/57AB2994831FFC5AA3B827DC40EE74AF/9781316514160AR.pdf/Monetary_Redress_for_Abuse_in_State_Care.pdf?event-type=FTLA"]," had an average wait time of 21 months."]},{"k":"p","s":["Compensation also remains comparatively low. New Zealand’s average payment of $30,000 is well below the roughly NZ$109,000 average payment under Australia’s ",["National Redress Scheme","https://www.nationalredress.gov.au/news/september-2025"]," and NZ$134,000 average ",["under Scotland’s redress scheme","https://www.gov.scot/publications/scotlands-redress-scheme-statistics-december-2021-june-2026/pages/outcomes-and-payments/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Honouring the promise"]},{"k":"p","s":["Looking ahead, best practice would involve ",["survivors leading the design","https://www.abuseinquiryresponse.govt.nz/about-us/official-information/information-releases/proactive-release-of-2"]," of an improved redress system, with a single, independent body that includes survivors of faith-based care and offers a flexible range of redress options."]},{"k":"p","s":["New Zealand could also consider establishing a prominent national memorial to survivors. While initiatives are underway to locate unmarked graves around the motu and establish smaller memorials at former institutional sites, there is a case for something more significant."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Australia’s national memorial","https://www.nca.gov.au/environment/projects/current-projects/national-memorial-victims-and-survivors"]," in Canberra offers one example. A suitable site might be found on the grounds of New Zealand’s parliament."]},{"k":"p","s":["Progress along the commission’s roadmap for redress has been slow. Long delays, limited compensation and the absence of an independent, survivor-led redress body show how much remains to be done."]},{"k":"p","s":["With this year’s anniversary falling amid post-election uncertainty, perhaps the best path forward lies in the Human Rights Commission’s “ ",["Honour the Promise","https://honourthepromise.co.nz/"],"” campaign, which calls on the government and other institutions responsible for abuse to implement all the commission’s recommendations."]},{"k":"p","s":["The government’s apology two years ago was an important moment of recognition. But for those 200,000 people who suffered abuse in care, acknowledgement must be met by renewed action."]}],"chars":5628},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sweden":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"1a54c6e7aa880a5d6926a5a924092a0f355d321c82cad03ea655da4007dd8727","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/sweden","bytes":61961,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Sweden travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-18T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-11T10:23:29+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sweden/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Sweden set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Swedish Embassy in the UK","https://www.swedenabroad.se/en/embassies/united-kingdom-london/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sweden follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport if you need to","https://www.gov.uk/apply-renew-passport"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["travel without a visa to the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/travel-to-eu-schengen-area"],", which includes Sweden, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["requirements for working in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-sweden-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day visa-free limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days. If you overstay the 90-day visa-free limit, you may be banned from entering Schengen countries for up to 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Swedish border control, you may also need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your accommodation, for example, a hotel booking"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your travel insurance"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, you must meet the Swedish government’s entry requirements. Check which type of visa or work permit you need with the ",["Swedish Embassy in the UK","https://www.swedenabroad.se/en/embassies/united-kingdom-london/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you stay in Sweden with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["entry processes if you live in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-sweden#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Border controls on arrival from Denmark and Germany"]},{"k":"p","s":["Border controls are in place in Sweden for people travelling from Denmark via the Öresund crossing and arriving on ferries from Denmark and Germany."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"p","s":["Carry a letter of consent from the parent or guardian of children aged 17 and under who are travelling with you."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Sweden guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/215/sweden#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods that you can take into and out of Sweden","https://www.tullverket.se/4.a62602917de14e432714e9.html"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food and drink into Sweden"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Sweden"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 euros or more. You will get a certified declaration to show you brought it in with you. If you do not, your money could be seized when you leave."]},{"k":"p","s":["Many shops, cafes and restaurants do not accept cash, so make sure you have a debit or credit card with you."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Sweden"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorists are likely to try to carry out attacks in Sweden."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places frequented by foreign nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Sweden have successfully disrupted a number of planned attacks and made a number of arrests."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations in Sweden are usually peaceful. Avoid demonstrations wherever possible and follow the advice of the local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take particular care of your belongings in major cities as pickpockets often target tourists for passports and cash."]},{"k":"p","s":["Violent crime does occur. Gang-related crime, including knife crime, shootings and explosions, has been reported in Malmö, Stockholm and Gothenburg."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Paying for sex is illegal, from 1 July 2025 this will also include the purchase of sexual acts online"]},{"k":"p","s":["Physical punishment of children is illegal."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, are strictly prohibited, even in small quantities. Severe penalties apply for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through airports. You should expect long jail sentences of up to 10 years and heavy fines."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Sweden have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is legal in Sweden. Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Sweden, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Sweden guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/sweden/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You need a UK driving licence to drive in Sweden. Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience and a minimum age."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Sweden, you may be able to use your ",["Blue Badge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/blue-badge-using-it-in-the-eu/using-a-blue-badge-in-the-european-union"]," in some circumstances. Check local parking rules."]},{"k":"p","s":["During winter, snow and ice on the roads cause accidents daily. From 1 December to 31 March, and any other times designated by the police, all vehicles must have winter tyres."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Arctic travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Parts of Sweden are in the Arctic Circle, including some very remote areas of land. Emergency medical assistance and search and rescue are limited in these areas. See ",["Arctic travel safety advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/arctic-travel-safety-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re visiting remote areas, consider how easily you will be able to access emergency services."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Sweden","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/215/sweden#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See what ",["health risks you’ll face in Sweden","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/215/sweden"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["biting insects and ticks"]},{"k":"li","s":["mpox – also see ",["Sweden’s Public Health Authority website","https://www.folkhalsomyndigheten.se/the-public-health-agency-of-sweden/communicable-disease-control/disease-information-about-mpox/one-case-of-mpox-clade-i-reported-in-sweden/"]," which includes updates on mpox"]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Sweden"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/sweden-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Sweden, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-in-sweden"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Sweden"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 112 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Sweden"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Sweden"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Sweden"]," in Sweden"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/deaths-in-sweden"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/sweden-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/sweden/british-embassy-stockholm"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sweden/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Sweden","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-sweden"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":13949,"change":"Updated information on European Entry-Exit System (EES) (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/slovakia":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"397adaa2cf44db1444114f9cc607877fdf707dbdce231375c773a040be719079","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/slovakia","bytes":78992,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Slovakia travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-11T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-28T14:01:26+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/slovakia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Slovakia’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Slovakia set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Slovak Embassy in the UK","https://www.mzv.sk/londyn-en"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Slovakia follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can travel without a visa to the Schengen area, which includes Slovakia, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day limit. Visits to Schengen countries in the 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, to work or study, for business travel or for other reasons, you must meet the local ",["foreigner residence requirements","https://www.mic.iom.sk/en/residence/general-information.html"]," and the ",["recent changes","https://www.mic.iom.sk/en/news/920-changes-in-the-act-on-the-residence-of-foreigners-from-july-1-2025.html"]," to this made on 1 July 2025."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in Slovakia with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["You could get a fine of up to 1,600 euros if you stay longer than legally allowed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["entry processes if you live in Slovakia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-slovakia#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["At border control in Slovakia, you may also need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove you have enough money for your stay"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your accommodation, for example, a hotel booking confirmation or proof of address for a second home"]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the police"]},{"k":"p","s":["As a visitor to Slovakia, you must ",["report where you are staying to the nearest Foreigners Police Department","https://www.minv.sk/?reporting-residence-1"],". If you stay at a hotel, the hotel will do this for you. If you are in private accommodation, you must notify the police by letter, email or in person within 3 working days of your arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to stay in Slovakia for a longer period, you must ",["apply for a residence permit","https://www.minv.sk/?residencies"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Slovakia","https://www.financnasprava.sk/en/individuals/customs-duty/import"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food into Slovakia"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Slovakia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 euros or more. You will get a certified declaration to show you brought it in with you. If you do not, your money could be seized when you leave."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Slovakia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Slovakia cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["An attack on a bar popular with the LGBT+ community in the capital, Bratislava, on 12 October 2022, which resulted in 2 deaths, was classified as a terrorist act. Prior to this attack, there was no recent history of terrorism in Slovakia."]},{"k":"h","s":["Border with Ukraine"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is widespread military activity in Ukraine. Border crossings with Ukraine may be affected by security measures, temporary restrictions or delays at short notice. If travelling near the Slovakia-Ukraine border, check local information before departure, follow instructions from Slovak police and border authorities. FCDO advises against travel to Ukraine. See warnings against travel in ",["Ukraine travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ukraine"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Political demonstrations can take place in Bratislava and other cities, sometimes at short notice. Recent demonstrations have generally been peaceful, but large gatherings may disrupt transport and public areas. Stay away from demonstrations, monitor local media, and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of petty crime, especially in Bratislava. Take precautions against pickpockets and bag snatchers and do not leave your possessions unattended. Pickpockets operate around the main tourist areas, particularly the Christmas markets and in bars, and have targeted foreign nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not leave your passport as a deposit for services such as car rental. Keep sight of your bank card when making payments."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking and scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of drink spiking and theft in clubs around the Bratislava Old Town pedestrian area. People have sometimes been charged extortionate prices for drinks, so check prices before ordering."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals sometimes target foreign-registered vehicles. They may puncture your tyres at a petrol station and then pretend to help or fake a breakdown. If you stop to help, they may rob you or steal your car."]},{"k":"p","s":["At night, only stop your car in a well-lit public area such as a service station. Make sure you lock your car and be extremely wary of anyone offering help."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not leave luggage in view in your car."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must always carry your passport with you as ID. You can get a fine of up to 1,600 euros for not showing ID if the Slovak police ask for it."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, carry severe penalties. You should expect a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Slovakia have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bratislava has become a popular destination for stag parties. Tourists have been fined or imprisoned for being a public nuisance. Unruly or rowdy behaviour is illegal could attract police attention, particularly between 10pm and 6am. In Bratislava Old Town, it is illegal to drink alcohol in the street. If you drink alcohol (for example, bought in a supermarket) in this or other public areas, you could get a fine of 33 euros."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Taking photos of any location that could be seen as a military establishment or security related may result in problems with the authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual relationships are legal in Slovakia, but Slovak law does not recognise same-sex marriages and civil partnerships. Same-sex couples showing affection in public may receive unwanted attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming and watersports"]},{"k":"p","s":["Observe local rules and regulations on publicly accessible lakes, rivers and other water sources. Jumping into unknown waters can result in serious injury, including paralysis or death."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Skiing and hiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re planning a winter sports holiday in Slovakia, see ",["Preparing for winter sports abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/winter-sports-stay-safe-on-the-slopes"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you need help from the ",["Slovak Mountain Rescue Service (HZS)","https://hzs.sk/kontakty/"],", you will have to meet costs of up to 1,000 euros. Make sure your insurance includes rescue costs. Anyone ignoring or violating HZS rules or instructions will get a fine of up to 3,320 euros."]},{"k":"p","s":["Brown bears are present in many mountain and forested areas, particularly in central and northern Slovakia, including parts of the Tatras, Low Tatras and Poľana areas. Bear sightings and occasional attacks have been reported. If hiking, stay on marked trails, avoid dense vegetation where visibility is poor, make noise in forested areas, keep dogs under close control, do not approach or feed wildlife, and check local warnings before setting out."]},{"k":"p","s":["For advice on minimising risks, see the ",["Slovak Wildlife Society","https://www.medvede.sk/index1.php?action=strety"]," website."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Slovakia, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Slovakia guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/slovak-republic/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Slovakia for up to 6 months. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["1968 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you stay longer than 6 months, you must ",["exchange your licence for a Slovak one","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-slovakia#driving-in-slovakia"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Slovakia, you may be able to use your ",["Blue Badge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/blue-badge-using-it-in-the-eu/using-a-blue-badge-in-the-european-union"]," in some circumstances. Check local parking rules."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, a higher minimum age and holding an IDP ."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Slovakia. If you are tested and found to have any alcohol in your system, you may get a fine of up to 1,300 euros, have your licence confiscated and face possible imprisonment."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Many main roads have only a single carriageway in each direction making overtaking difficult. Beware of oncoming cars overtaking on your side of the road. Road markings are difficult to see in poor weather and can be faded. In winter, you must equip your car for severe driving conditions."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tolls"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you use Slovak motorways (‘Dialnica’), you must display a motorway vignette (sticker) on the inside of your windscreen as you enter Slovakia. If you do not, you could get a heavy fine. You can get a motorway vignette at all major border crossings into Slovakia and at larger petrol stations. More information and a road network map is available from the ",["toll system operator","https://www.emyto.sk/en/etoll/etc-system"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Bratislava Old Town and in nightlife areas, check prices before ordering, keep your drink in sight, use licensed taxis or reputable ride-hailing services. If you feel threatened, leave the area and contact police. See information on ",["local taxi use","https://www.visitbratislava.com/informations/transport/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/extreme-weather-and-natural-hazards"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Slovakia can experience extreme weather, including heatwaves, storms, heavy rain, flash flooding, winter snow and mountain weather that changes quickly."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check ",["local forecasts and weather warnings","https://www.shmu.sk/sk/?page=987"]," in Slovakia and follow the instructions of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 155 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Slovakia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/197/slovakia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See what ",["health risks you’ll face in Slovakia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/197/slovakia"],", including an increased risk of tick bites from April to October."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Slovakia. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Slovakia"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Slovakia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/slovakia-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Slovakia, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Slovakia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-in-slovakia"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Slovakia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 155"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 150"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 158"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Slovakia"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find?serviceType=funeralDirectors&readNotice=ok&insurance=no&repatriation=yes&country=Slovakia"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/slovakia-list-of-translators-and-interpreters"]," in Slovakia"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Slovakia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/slovakia-bereavement-pack-information"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Slovakia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/slovakia-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/slovakia/british-embassy-bratislava"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/slovakia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Slovakia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-slovakia"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":18679,"change":"This travel advice has been updated for accuracy with changes made throughout, including border with Ukraine, political demonstrations, skiing and hiking, taxi’s, and extreme weather and natural disasters (‘Safety and security page’)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/trinidad-and-tobago":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T17:18Z","sha":"58711ceda22dba310f704cedbcfa35b4b6f1a6cf5931196047a583adf199ce60","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/trinidad-and-tobago","bytes":64409,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Trinidad and Tobago travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-30T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-11T16:31:16+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and get ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/trinidad-and-tobago/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Trinidad and Tobago’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Trinidad and Tobago set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Trinidad and Tobago High Commission in the UK","https://foreign.gov.tt/missions-consuls/tt-missions-abroad/diplomatic-missions/high-commission-london-united-kingdom/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Trinidad and Tobago your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Trinidad and Tobago without a visa for up to 90 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have:"]},{"k":"li","s":["an onward or return ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["proof of funds for your stay"]},{"k":"p","s":["For business or study, see visa requirements on the ",["Trinidad and Tobago immigration division website","https://nationalsecurity.gov.tt/divisions/immigrationdivision/"]," or contact the ",["Trinidad and Tobago High Commission in the UK","https://foreign.gov.tt/missions-consuls/tt-missions-abroad/diplomatic-missions/high-commission-london-united-kingdom/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get your 90-day visit extended by ",["applying to the passport and immigration department","https://nationalsecurity.gov.tt/faq/how-to-apply-for-an-extension-of-stay-in-trinidad-and-tobago/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you get your passport stamped."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you do not have an entry stamp in your passport, it will be difficult to extend your stay."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a returning resident, you may be asked to show your residence permit or work permit when you arrive in Trinidad and Tobago."]},{"k":"h","s":["Arrival/Departure Card"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter or exit Trinidad and Tobago you must complete the online Arrival/Departure Card."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will have access to the portal 72 hours before arrival or departure."]},{"k":"p","s":["See requirements on the ",["Trinidad and Tobago Online Immigration & Customs Portal","https://travel.gov.tt/en"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry by sea"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can sail into and out of the marinas or Chaguaramas Bay. You must follow government regulations or you could be stopped by the coast guard, who will hand you over to the police."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about health entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Trinidad and Tobago guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/225/trinidad-and-tobago#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Trinidad and Tobago","https://tradeind.gov.tt/tlu/"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Trinidad and Tobago"]},{"k":"p","s":["The amount of currency you can bring in (or take out of) the country is unlimited. However you must declare cash in any currency worth over 20,000 Trinidad and Tobago dollars. There are strict penalties for failing to declare amounts in excess of the limit."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Trinidad and Tobago"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there is no recent history of terrorism in Trinidad and Tobago, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Violent crime and assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Trinidad and Tobago, there is a high level of violent crime, including gang-related attacks and shootings. Instances of homicides and armed robberies are common."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be cautious particularly when travelling in or around:"]},{"k":"li","s":["downtown Port of Spain (east of Charlotte street, south and west of Picadilly street, and north of Independence Square)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Carenage, Sea Lots, Belmont, Laventille, Morvant and Beetham"]},{"k":"li","s":["Barataria and Cocorite"]},{"k":"li","s":["the interior of Queens Park Savannah"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals may carry out attacks, ranging from the threat of violence to sexual assault and robbery, kidnapping for ransom, rape and murder. A significant portion of violent crime in Trinidad and Tobago is gang-related. You can take precautions against being targeted:"]},{"k":"li","s":["always be alert to your surroundings - do not wear headphones when out and about"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking or hiking alone, deserted beaches or isolated areas"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid poorly lit or quiet areas of streets or parks"]},{"k":"li","s":["consider removing expensive jewellery"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep money, valuables and passports somewhere safe"]},{"k":"li","s":["park in well-lit areas"]},{"k":"li","s":["never leave anything in an unattended vehicle"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not leave bags, purses, handbags, laptops and phones exposed on the seat of a moving vehicle - there have been incidents of items being snatched through car windows"]},{"k":"li","s":["be careful when withdrawing cash from ATMs or changing money - use well-lit ATMs, withdraw small amounts only and preferably during daylight hours"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep an eye on your card when making purchases to avoid card skimming"]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re staying in a villa, especially in an isolated area, make sure it has adequate security, including external security lighting, grills and overnight security guards"]},{"k":"li","s":["be cautious about what you post online - social media platforms are increasingly being monitored by criminals"]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re attacked or robbed, don’t resist - criminals are often armed, and you can be seriously injured or killed"]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re the victim of a violent crime, especially rape, get urgent medical help"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drug assisted robbery and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports of criminals in Trinidad and Tobago using drugs, deception and sexual solicitation, including via dating apps, to facilitate robbery and sexual assault. Victims may be lured and drugged or beaten and robbed. Incidents have occurred in bars, nightclubs, hotels and on city streets, sometimes involving groups working together."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid accepting invitations from strangers or inviting them to your accommodation"]},{"k":"li","s":["take care when using dating apps"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep food and drinks under supervision"]},{"k":"li","s":["plan travel routes in advance, particularly at night"]},{"k":"p","s":["If drugged, seek local medical attention as soon as possible."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Carnival and other large-scale events"]},{"k":"p","s":["Robberies and opportunistic crime may occur during the carnival period. If you are attending a large-scale event, such as the Carnival celebrations in Trinidad, you should take sensible precautions and always be aware of your surroundings. Avoid displaying jewellery or valuable items that may attract attention of criminals. Practice caution at crowded events and have a clear plan to return home safely after attending any events."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking"]},{"k":"p","s":["Avoid accepting drinks from anyone you do not know, and do not leave drinks unattended."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"p","s":["Providers of recreational and adventure tourism may not meet UK safety standards. Check safety standards and make sure you’re insured if you take part in activities like boat trips, water sports, quad biking or horse riding."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Laws on clothing"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is an offence for anyone, including children, to dress in camouflage clothing or bring any into the country."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs penalties"]},{"k":"p","s":["Trinidad and Tobago has decriminalised the possession of up to 30g of cannabis or up to 5g of cannabis resin. However, you’re not allowed to smoke cannabis in public spaces or while operating a vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are stopped with these amounts, you can be fined up to 50,000 Trinidad and Tobago dollars. For larger quantities there are more hefty fines and possible imprisonment. There are also severe penalties for other drug-related offences."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["In March 2025, consensual same-sex activity was reinstated as a crime punishable by up to 5 years imprisonment. It is also illegal for LGBT+ foreign nationals to enter the country. However, neither of these laws has been enforced in recent times. It is possible LGBT+ visitors could receive negative attention if they show affection in public."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care when using dating apps especially those used by the LGBT+ community. Criminals have used apps like Grindr to trick, rob and assault people."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Trespassing"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter private property without permission, you may face a fine of up to 750,000 Trinidad and Tobago Dollars or imprisonment for up to 25 years. Local laws allow legal occupants of private property to use defensive and even deadly force against trespassers."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals may carry out attacks in vehicles. You can take precautions against being targeted by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["using well-established taxi firms, recommended by locals or your hotel"]},{"k":"li","s":["being cautious about accepting lifts in a private car or ‘maxi taxi’"]},{"k":"li","s":["taking local advice to avoid areas affected by gang violence"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping the windows closed and doors locked while driving"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Trinidad and Tobago, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Trinidad and Tobago for up to 90 days. If you still have a paper driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["1949 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"],") as well. You’ll need an IDP if you stay longer than 90 days."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving standards and road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving standards in Trinidad and Tobago are mixed. High-speed road accidents on the main highways in Trinidad often result in fatalities. There have been accidents caused by drink-driving, including on the Beetham-Churchill Roosevelt Highway to and from the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some roads are narrow and winding, and road surfaces are of a mixed standard. Pedestrians often walk on the roads, and drivers do not always indicate. Take care when driving and observe speed limits."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take extra care when driving at night as some roads are unlit. Road signs and hazards may not be easily visible."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hurricanes"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Caribbean hurricane season normally runs from June to November. Trinidad and Tobago is rarely affected by hurricanes, but can have severe storms. You can monitor local and international weather updates from the US National Hurricane Center and check the ",["World Meteorological Organisation weather reports for Trinidad and Tobago","https://worldweather.wmo.int/en/country.html?countryCode=27"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes are a risk in Trinidad and Tobago. The last serious earthquake was in 2018 and caused damage to some buildings and communication networks. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanic eruptions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Monitor the ",["alert level of ‘Kick’em Jenny’","https://safetyandsecuritynet.org/tag/kick-em-jenny/"],", an underwater volcano located off the coast of Grenada and about 200km from Trinidad. It erupts every few years. Follow the advice of the local authorities if there is increased activity."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 811 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Trinidad and Tobago","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/225/trinidad-and-tobago#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The yellow fever vaccination is recommended for some travellers if visiting Trinidad – see ",["TravelHealthPro’s vaccine recommendations","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/225/trinidad-and-tobago#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what other health risks you’ll face in Trinidad and Tobago","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/225/trinidad-and-tobago#Other_Risks"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The 2022 UNAIDS Country Factsheet estimated that around 12,000 adults aged 15 or over in Trinidad and Tobago were living with HIV. Take precautions to avoid exposure to HIV."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Trinidad and Tobago"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities can be limited in some areas of Trinidad and Tobago. Private clinics can treat most ordinary problems, but medical evacuation may be necessary in more serious cases. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Trinidad and Tobago","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/trinidad-and-tobago-list-of-medical-facilities-practioners"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Trinidad and Tobago"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 811"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 990"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 999"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=trinidad%20and%20tobago"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Trinidad%20and%20Tobago"]," in Trinidad and Tobago"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Trinidad and Tobago","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/what-to-do-after-a-british-person-dies-in-trinidad-and-tobago"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Trinidad and Tobago","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/trinidad-and-tobago-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/trinidad-and-tobago/british-high-commission-port-of-spain"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/trinidad-and-tobago/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Trinidad and Tobago","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-trinidad-tobago"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":15483,"change":"New information about Arrival/Departure card (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/the-last-bird-to-go-extinct-in-nz-has-lessons-for-saving-other-species-new-study-292112":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-18T20:25Z","sha":"d4a39896fede9aea3cf7174710cb45606bd9b2f168815498ebbf977b03cdcc1a","read":"https://theconversation.com/the-last-bird-to-go-extinct-in-nz-has-lessons-for-saving-other-species-new-study-292112","bytes":186087,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"The last bird to go extinct in NZ has lessons for saving other species – new study","by":[{"n":"Nic Rawlence","r":"Associate Professor in Ancient DNA, University of Otago"},{"n":"Alexander Verry","r":"Researcher, Department of Zoology, University of Otago"}],"pub":"2026-11-18T18:56:04Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["The last bird to go extinct in Aotearoa New Zealand was a tiny wren that disappeared from a small island off the southern tip of Rakiura Stewart Island around 1972."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Stead’s bush wren","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/bush-wren"],", or mātuhituhi, was the last of its kind. The ",["North and South Island bush wrens","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/bush-wren"]," had already gone extinct by 1955 and 1968, respectively. Stead’s bush wren followed after ship rats invaded its last stronghold on Taukihepa Big South Cape Island."]},{"k":"p","s":["This incursion also caused the extinction of the tutukiwi ",["Stewart Island snipe","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/south-island-snipe"]," and ",["greater short-tailed bat","https://www.doc.govt.nz/nature/native-animals/bats-pekapeka/short-tailed-bat/"],", as well as the near-extinction of the ",["South Island tīeke saddleback","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/south-island-saddleback"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The rats were likely introduced accidentally from fishing boats around 1963 and exterminated the wrens rapidly within a few years. Six birds were moved to nearby Kaimohu Island in 1964, but despite the best efforts to save them, the last two individuals were seen in 1972."]},{"k":"p","s":["The extinction of Stead’s bush wren shows how ",["vulnerable small ground-dwelling birds are","https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/afternoons/collections/critter-of-the-week/audio/2019050517/critter-of-the-week-matuhituhi-stead-s-bush-wren"],". But scientists know surprisingly ",["little about them","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/assets/95597/1691915866-305_bush-20wren.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Now, our ",["new study based on ancient DNA","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1055790326002113"]," reveals the evolutionary and ecological history of these lost birds as well as a previously unrecognised member of the bush wren family."]},{"k":"h","s":["Deep and dynamic evolutionary history"]},{"k":"p","s":["Bush wrens (Xenicus longipes) were once widespread across New Zealand, with different subspecies on the North, South and Stewart Island. The historical and fossil record shows they were a forest specialist with limited dispersal ability."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bush wrens belong to New Zealand’s unique wren family (acanthisittidae) that also includes the living pīwauwau ",["rock wren","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/rock-wren"]," and tītipounamu ",["rifleman","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/rifleman"]," as well as four other ",["recently extinct species","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/searchGroup?q=Acanthisittidae"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["New Zealand wrens are representatives of an ancient lineage which diverged from all other song birds (passerines) about ",["70 million years ago","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1055790316301270"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Our ",["analysis of ancient DNA","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1055790326002113"]," from specimens held in museum collections worldwide shows bush wrens evolved about six million years ago when the uplift of the Southern Alps created a variety of new habitats."]},{"k":"p","s":["The North (Xenicus stokesii) and South Island (Xenicus longipes) bush wren diverged about 2.6 million years ago. Back then, the southern end of the North Island was connected to the South Island and as marine straits in the Manawatu region narrowed, bush wrens could island hop from south to north. Our analyses strongly suggest they should be recognised as distinct species rather than subspecies."]},{"k":"p","s":["South Island bush wrens remained stable until about 500,000 years ago when we identified a near simultaneous, major diversification event that split them into northern, central and southern lineages. Stead’s bush wren emerged about 94,000 years ago."]},{"k":"h","s":["A new member of the bush wren family"]},{"k":"p","s":["The key to disentangling the evolutionary history of bush wrens was our ability to use ancient DNA to ",["genetically sex specimens","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1055790326002113"]," and to re-examine the plumage of male and female museum specimens. Prior to this, scientists were convinced there were ",["no plumage differences","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/assets/95597/1691915866-305_bush-20wren.pdf"]," between the sexes."]},{"k":"p","s":["We found that different bush wren lineages do in fact show marked plumage differences between sexes, with the exception of the South Island bush wren from Fiordland, where males and females both had green plumage."]},{"k":"p","s":["When we put all the genetic and plumage data together, we found a new bush wren hiding in plain sight within the central and northern South Island. It was different to all the other recognised bush wrens."]},{"k":"p","s":["We named this new subspecies the Tōtaranui bush wren (Xenicus longipes perditus) after the Māori name for Queen Charlotte Sound. In its Latin name, perditus stands for lost."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ice Age climate change"]},{"k":"p","s":["What caused this near simultaneous burst of diversification in bush wrens?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some 2.58 million years ago, the world entered an ",["Ice Age","https://theconversation.com/loss-of-forests-brought-new-birds-to-nz-during-the-last-ice-age-were-witnessing-a-similar-process-now-248523"]," and cycled through 19 to 20 shifts between cold glacial and warm interglacial periods. Forests and the animals that lived in them ",["retreated into separate areas during cold periods and expanded across the South Island when it warmed","https://theconversation.com/how-did-ancient-moa-survive-the-ice-age-and-what-can-they-teach-us-about-modern-climate-change-183350"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Initially, these cycles were relatively mild, lasting around 40,000 years. However, when Earth’s wobble on its axis shifted around one millions years ago, these cycles intensified and extended, lasting about 100,000 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["While bush wrens could buffer climatic changes to an extent, they eventually reached a tipping point and ",["retreated to multiple different refugia","https://blogs.otago.ac.nz/lost-worlds/2021/03/17/climate-refugee-or-hardy-local-solving-a-botanical-mystery/"],". Their diversification is a prime example of ",["glacier-driven evolution","https://theconversation.com/a-new-method-of-extracting-ancient-dna-from-tiny-bones-reveals-the-hidden-evolutionary-history-of-new-zealand-geckos-180327"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The extinction of the ",["Stead’s bush wren","https://www.nzbirdsonline.org.nz/species/bush-wren"]," marked a ",["turning point","https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/afternoons/collections/critter-of-the-week/audio/2019050517/critter-of-the-week-matuhituhi-stead-s-bush-wren"]," for New Zealand conservation and the management of rodents on islands. Yet bush wrens continue to ",["provide lessons","https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1055790326002113"]," for future ecosystem restoration and conservation management, especially for species with strict habitat requirements."]},{"k":"p","s":["Accelerating human-driven environmental impacts and climate change will put ecosystems under increased pressure and exacerbate the impacts of invasive species and habitat modification."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is imperative we do what we can now to preserve genetic diversity within threatened species and increase the climate resilience of ecosystems before it is too late."]}],"chars":5033}};
